## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model nonstationary, real-world acoustics with online Bayesian tracking and performance under sensor dropouts.

## Key Concepts

- Bayesian online convolutive ICA for time-varying mixing
- Online whitening and fixed-point demixing under nonstationarity
- Handling mic dropouts and strict latency constraints
- Ground-truth SDR/SIR evaluation in dynamic environments

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for online update (high level)
function update(W, X, prior) {
  // whitening
  const Xw = whiten(X);
  // track M(t) via prior
  const M = smoothTrack(W, prior);
  // update demixing
  W = demixUpdate(Xw, M);
  return W;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt the model for moving objects vs. stationary sources?
- What priors best capture rapid, abrupt environmental changes and how would you validate them?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests robustness to intermittent channel loss, online adaptation to changing source counts, and practical trade-offs between per-bin ICA and joint diagonalization under partial observability.

## Key Concepts
- Partial observability in BSS with channel dropouts
- Online whitening with forgetting factors
- Dynamic choice: per-bin ICA vs joint diagonalization
- Subspace completion to synthesize missing channels
- Cross-bin permutation under drift and speaker tracking

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch: update loop for online ICA with dropout handling
while (streaming) {
  x = read4Channels();
  W = updateWhitening(x);
  A = chooseDemixer(W, x, dropoutMask);
  s = A * x;
  updatePermutation(s);
}
```

